/*==============================================================
FILE:         _project_main.do
PROJECT:      Weather Changes, Ungulate Populations, & Vehicle Collisions
CURRENT LEAD: Wendy Wang (maintained jointly with Nicole Martinez)

PURPOSE:      Master script. Calls every stage of the project's pipeline in
              the order it has to run: weather extraction, the merged
              county-year panel, the descriptive exhibits, the regressions,
              and the tables that go to Overleaf.

CHANGELOG:
  09/11/2026 Wendy Wang: Wired in the weather pipeline scripts (02a, 04a, 05, 06) 
  & the descriptive exhibits scripts (09a, 09b, 10).
  09/15/2026 Wendy Wang: updated after first pass of weather-collision and 
  weather-wildlife regression analysis.
    - Added population scripts (08a then 08b) and build_main_data_county_year.do
    to the Preparing Data section.
    - Filled in Regression Estimation and Tables & Figures section; the Earth Engine
    extracts and Collisions & Ungulate Repo append scripts are left commented.
==============================================================*/

*---------------------------------------------------------------
* SECTION 0: SETUP
*---------------------------------------------------------------

cap log close
clear all
set more off, permanently
set matsize 11000
set maxvar 32767
set scheme s1mono

* Kodama is tried first: it is where the data live and where the pipeline is
* actually run. The Windows branches below are the style guide's template,
* kept so the file still resolves on a laptop.
confirmdir "/mnt/data_d/Dropbox/Research"
if r(confirmdir) == "0" {
    local rootDir = "/mnt/data_d/Dropbox/Research"
}
confirmdir "C:/Dropbox"
if r(confirmdir) == "0" {
    local rootDir = "C:/Dropbox/Research"
}
confirmdir "D:/Dropbox"
if r(confirmdir) == "0" {
    local rootDir = "D:/Dropbox/Research"
}

if "`rootDir'" == "" {
    di as error "No Dropbox research root found. Add this machine's " ///
                "root to the confirmdir block above."
    exit 601
}

if "$path" == "" {
    global path = "`rootDir'/AnimalCollisionsWeather"
}

* Convenience globals, all subpaths of $path. Every child .do re-derives its
* own from $path, so these are for this file's own use and for any script that
* chooses to read them rather than rebuild them.
global codePYTHON "$path/codePYTHON"
global codeSTATA  "$path/codeSTATA"
global dataCSV    "$path/dataCSV"
global dataRAW    "$path/dataRAW"
global dataSTATA  "$path/dataSTATA"
global estimates  "$path/dataSTATA/estimates"
global figures    "$path/figures"
global tables     "$path/tables"
global reports    "$path/reports"
global pathUngulates  "`rootDir'/UngulatePopulationDataRepo"
global pathCollisions "`rootDir'/VehicleCollisionsDataRepo"

* Named log. Every child .do opens its own log and starts with an unnamed
* `cap log close`, which would close this one if it were unnamed too.
cap mkdir "$codeSTATA/logs"
cap log close main
log using "$codeSTATA/logs/_project_main.log", replace text name(main)

/* Python interpreter used for all `python script` calls below.
   Uncomment and point at the project environment so the version that produced
   the results is documented. `python query` shows what is set now. */

* python set exec "/mnt/data_d/Dropbox/Research/AnimalCollisionsWeather/.venv/bin/python", permanently

/*
    Add files in the order they need to run, especially in the data building
    part.

    To run Stata, R, and Python scripts, use:

        do "$path/codeSTATA/<script_name>.do"

        rscript using "$path/codeSTATA/<script_name>.R"

        python script "$path/codePYTHON/<script_name>.py"

        where <script_name> is the file name you're trying to run

*/

*---------------------------------------------------------------
* SECTION 1: USER-WRITTEN PACKAGES
*---------------------------------------------------------------

/*
    Installed once per machine, not on every run. The estimation and report
    scripts each re-check the handful they depend on (reghdfe, ftools, require,
    estout, texdoc) and install what is missing, so a fresh Stata will get
    through the pipeline without this block being run by hand first.

    ssc install coefplot
    ssc install estout
    ssc install confirmdir
    ssc install unique
    ssc install egenmore
    ssc install freqindex
    ssc install matchit
    ssc install clustse
    ssc install parmest
    ssc install tmpdir
    ssc install sutex
    ssc install synth
    ssc install binscatter
    ssc install _gwtmean
    ssc install spmap
    ssc install shp2dta
    ssc install geoinpoly
    ssc install geo2xy
    ssc install mif2dta
    ssc install ftools
    ssc install moremata
    ssc install ivreg2
    ssc install reghdfe
    ssc install ppmlhdfe
    ssc install require
    ssc install texdoc
    ssc install acreg

    mata: mata mlib index

    net install rscript, from("https://raw.githubusercontent.com/reifjulian/rscript") replace

    Python packages are pinned in codePYTHON/requirements.txt:
        pip install -r "$path/codePYTHON/requirements.txt"
*/

*---------------------------------------------------------------
* SECTION 2: PREPARING DATA
*---------------------------------------------------------------

* ---------- 2.1 Weather extraction (Earth Engine) -----------

* Commented out on purpose. Each of these is a multi-hour Earth Engine export
* covering all CONUS counties, 1981-2025, and both have already been run to
* completion on the Kodama server -- their outputs, 
* dataCSV/PRISM/prism_county_month.csv and dataCSV/ERA5/era5_county_month.csv, 
* are what 05 reads. Re-enable only to rebuild the extracts from scratch, 
* which also needs a valid Earth Engine credential (00_setup_earth_engine.py) 
* and Google Drive space for the export.

* python script "$codePYTHON/02a_extract_prism_county.py"
* python clear

* python script "$codePYTHON/04a_extract_era5_county.py"
* python clear

* [PLACEHOLDER] WMA-level extraction (/codePYTHON scripts 02b, 04b), 
* pending WMA shapefiles.

* ------ 2.2 Weather aggregation and derived variables -------

python script "$codePYTHON/05_aggregate_daily_to_monthly.py"
python clear

python script "$codePYTHON/06_build_derived_weather_vars.py"
python clear

* ------------------ 2.3 County population -------------------
* separate script for CT b/c it uses planning regions instead of counties. 
* 08b's CT override reads 08a's output (ct_population_county_from_towns.csv) 
* and raises if it isn't there yet.
python script "$codePYTHON/08a_population_ct_towns.py"
python clear

python script "$codePYTHON/08b_population_county.py"
python clear

* -------- 2.4 Upstream panels from the other two RAs --------

/* Both of these live in sibling repos and neither is this project's to run:
   each writes into its own repo's tree, and each is owned by the RA who
   wrote it. The merge in 2.5 reads the .dta they produce, already staged
   into this project:

       $dataSTATA/US_deer_harvest_county_year_04sep2026.dta
       $dataRAW/Collisions/collisions_CONUS_county_year_1985_2020.dta

   Left commented until Nicole and Charvi confirm these are the right entry
   points, that running them from here is safe, and how the output should be
   copied across. The collisions snapshot in particular predates Charvi's
   2019-2020 updates, so wiring this up is also what un-freezes the
   collisions sample.

   Nicole's per-state harmonization files under $pathUngulates/codeSTATA/<ST>/
   run before the append; Charvi's per-state files under
   $pathCollisions/codeSTATA/data_dvcs_county_year_split/ run before hers. */

* do "$pathUngulates/codeSTATA/deer_harvest_national_append.do"
* do "$pathCollisions/codeSTATA/data_append_state_collisions_files.do"

* ---------- 2.5 Merged county-year analysis panel -----------

* Merges PRISM, ERA5 snow, population, collisions and wildlife harvest onto
* the weather spine, and builds the four candidate winter regressors
* (mean_winter_temp, warm_winter_1sd/2sd, wsi_cold_days,
* winter_severity_index). Writes $dataSTATA/main_data_county_year.dta, which
* is the input to everything in SECTIONS 3, 4 and 5.
*
* Merges here deliberately keep every row rather than asserting _merge == 3,
* per Eyal (09/01/2026): counties present in collisions or wildlife but absent
* from the weather panel are tracked, not troubleshot. SECTION 8 of that file
* exports the unmatched-year diagnostics that stand in for the assert.

do "$codeSTATA/build_main_data_county_year.do"

* Standalone diagnostic: confirms the all-animal collision count is not
* silently standing in for the deer-only count anywhere it should not.
* Writes $tables/checks/animal_deer_backfill_violations.csv and nothing else.

do "$codeSTATA/check_animal_deer_backfill.do"

*---------------------------------------------------------------
* SECTION 3: DESCRIPTIVE DATA ANALYSIS
*---------------------------------------------------------------

/* Weather descriptives. Tier 1 is internal QA; Tier 2 is the exhibit set that
   10_generate_weather_report.do knits into the dated PDF report. Both are
   generated from codePYTHON/09_descriptive_weather_full.ipynb by
   codePYTHON/make_scripts.py -- edit the notebook, then regenerate the two
   scripts; do not edit 09a or 09b by hand.

   09b must run before 10, which asserts that every exhibit it expects exists.

   ORDERING NOTE (09/10/2026, addressed here 09/15/2026). One exhibit in 09b,
   the Winter Severity Index choropleth, READS winter_severity_index from
   $dataSTATA/main_data_county_year.dta rather than computing it, because
   SECTION 7 of build_main_data_county_year.do is the single place the
   project's four winter measures are constructed and a second definition in
   Python is exactly the drift that produced the warm-anomaly conflict.

   That made a Phase 4 output depend on a Phase 6 input. While the merge was
   unwired and this section ran first, a clean end-to-end run left the .dta
   absent: 09b printed a NOTE, skipped the two WSI exhibits, and 10 omitted
   that section -- the report still built, one section short, quietly. Running
   the merge in SECTION 2.5 above is what closes that, and it is where the
   merge belongs anyway, being data preparation.

   That is an ordering fix, not the real fix. The index is PRISM cold days
   plus ERA5 snow days -- pure weather inputs, both already in the derived-vars
   CSVs 09b reads -- so it should be built in 06_build_derived_weather_vars.py
   and READ by the merge, which would also point the dependency the right way.
   Deferred to the code-review pass rather than done here: it touches a script
   the PI has signed off and that Tables 4 and 5 were run against. When doing
   it, keep the Kohn 1975 / WI DNR definition exactly as SECTION 7 has it and
   carry the _snow12 / _snow8 sensitivity variants. */

python script "$codePYTHON/09a_descriptive_weather_tier1.py"
python clear

python script "$codePYTHON/09b_descriptive_weather_tier2.py"
python clear

do "$codeSTATA/10_generate_weather_report.do"

*---------------------------------------------------------------
* SECTION 4: REGRESSION ESTIMATION
*---------------------------------------------------------------

* --------- 4.1 Collisions on winter weather (Wendy) ---------

* Estimation only. Writes the 40-file .ster grid under
* $estimates/collisions_weather/ --
* <outcome>_p<A|B1|B2|C|D>_c<1-4>_W<none|pop>.ster, two outcomes
* (animal_share, animal_rate_100k) x five panels x four columns -- plus
* _run_settings.txt, which 5.1 reads back so the two files cannot drift.
* Split from the table file per Eyal (09/08/2026): estimation is slow on this
* panel, and a script that does one thing is easier to read.
*
* The specification switches (winter lag, share numerator, share scale,
* weights) are SECTION 1 of that file, and are set there, not here.

do "$codeSTATA/estimates_generate_collisions_weather.do"

* ------- 4.2 Deer harvest on winter weather (Nicole) --------

* Unlike 4.1, each of these three estimates AND writes its own table, so they
* sit here rather than in SECTION 5. Outputs are $tables/wildlife_weather/
* regression_<name>.tex plus a .csv mirror of each, and they are Tables 1, 2
* and 3 of the working paper in this order: log(harvest_total), harvest per
* 1,000 residents, and the within-county z-score of harvest.
*
* These three currently set their own root globals rather than reading $path.
* They resolve to the same tree on Kodama, so the pipeline runs correctly, but
* it means $path set here does not reach them. Raised with Nicole for the code
* review; not changed here, since they are her files.

do "$codeSTATA/regression_log_harvest.do"

do "$codeSTATA/regression_harvest_per1000.do"

do "$codeSTATA/regression_harvest_zscore.do"

*---------------------------------------------------------------
* SECTION 5: MAIN ANALYSIS TABLES & FIGURES
*---------------------------------------------------------------

* ---------- 5.1 Collision tables (Tables 4 and 5) -----------

* Reads the .ster grid from 4.1 back with `estimates use`, exports each panel
* with estout, and wraps the four blocks into one table with texdoc. Writes
* $tables/collisions_weather/table_collisions_weather_{share,rate}.tex and the
* four per-panel fragments each of them \ExpandableInput's.
*
* Which anomaly threshold is published is the panelB_sd switch inside that
* file: 1sigma and 2sigma are estimated separately in 4.1 because 2sigma is a
* subset of 1sigma, and the notes record that the other was estimated too.

do "$codeSTATA/estimates_tables_collisions_weather.do"

* ---------- 5.2 Where the other exhibits come from ----------

* Tables 1-3 (harvest) are written by the three files in 4.2, which do their
* own export. The Tier 2 weather exhibits and the dated PDF report are written
* in SECTION 3. Nothing else is generated here.

*---------------------------------------------------------------
* SECTION 6: ROBUSTNESS CHECKS
*---------------------------------------------------------------



*---------------------------------------------------------------
* END
*---------------------------------------------------------------

log close main
